---
import MapContentLayout from '../components/multiple-maps/MapContentLayout.astro';
---

<MapContentLayout title="Create a Map With Multiple Pins – Free Online Tool" heading="Create a Map With Multiple Pins" description="Try a three-pin New York example, see how coordinates and pin order work, and open an editable copy in the free map generator." intro="Try plotting a New York meetup with three pins. Each point has an exact position and a short instruction. Open an editable copy to change the order, add your own place, or see what visitors will find when they open a pin.">
  <div class="mm-reading">
    <a class="btn-primary" href="/multiple-location-map-generator/?example=nyc#location-editor">Open the three-pin example</a>
    <h2>Try the Three-Pin Meetup</h2>
    <details><summary>1. Central Park</summary><p><code>40.77360, -73.97080</code>. Meet at Bethesda Terrace. Use an exact coordinate when a park name covers a large area.</p></details>
    <details><summary>2. New York Public Library</summary><p><code>40.75320, -73.98220</code>. Start at the Fifth Avenue entrance. A street address helps visitors tell branches apart.</p></details>
    <details><summary>3. Brooklyn Bridge</summary><p><code>40.70610, -73.99690</code>. Use the pedestrian walkway. Mark the approach people need if the landmark has several entrances.</p></details>
    <h2>Replace a Pin With Your Own Location</h2>
    <p>In the example, select <strong>Delete</strong> next to a place you don’t need. Click the map or enter coordinates for the replacement. If address search is available, you can also look up the place and select the result that matches its city or street. Use <strong>Move up</strong> and <strong>Move down</strong> to put the new pin in the right order.</p>
    <h2>Plot a Fresh Set of Pins</h2>
    <p>Open the <a href="/multiple-location-map-generator/">blank generator</a> and select <strong>Add Multiple Locations</strong>. Paste one address per line. Add <code>|</code> after an address to attach a note, then preview and confirm the rows. Keep numbered pins if the order matters. Use a standard pin or circle for a place that people can visit independently.</p>
    <h2>Share Your Example or Add It to a Website</h2>
    <p>Save your version to get a shareable map URL and an iframe. Send the URL to attendees or paste the iframe into a custom HTML block on your event page. Open the public URL on your phone and check that the meetup instructions are readable in all three popups.</p>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details><summary>Can two pins share an address?</summary><p>Yes. Use coordinates to distinguish separate entrances. If pins are still close together, clustering groups them until the viewer zooms in or opens the group.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does the pin order draw a route?</summary><p>The number shows the order you chose. Visitors can open each pin independently, and the map does not calculate a walking or driving route.</p></details>
    <p>For a full publishing walkthrough, see <a href="/guides/how-to-create-map-with-multiple-pins/">how to create a map with multiple pins</a>.</p>
  </div>
</MapContentLayout>
